---
import ProjectCase from "../../layouts/ProjectCase.astro";

const figmaFileUrl =
  "https://www.figma.com/design/fDDMxTuLowUBqhodkOqYDU/An%C3%A1lise-de-Risco-App?node-id=380-8093";

const project = {
  title: "Comgás Field Safety App",
  client: "Comgás (via Exed Consulting)",
  year: "2024–2025",
  role: "Product Designer (sole designer)",
  platform: "Mobile app + web",
  summary:
    "A mobile and web safety workflow, developed and delivered for Comgás, that replaced paper-based Risk Analysis and Work Permit forms — guiding technicians through eight high-risk scenarios in the field and giving supervisors and office teams a structured record to approve and review.",

  heroImage: "/images/comgas/cover-opt.jpg",
  heroAlt: "Comgás field safety app case study cover",
  hideOverview: true,

  challenge: [
    "Field technicians relied on paper forms to register risks and request work permits — slow, hard to review, easy to lose.",
  ],

  solution: [],

  sections: [
    {
      id: "scan",
      kind: "scan",
      eyebrow: "30-second read",
      scanItems: [
        {
          title: "Problem",
          body: "Risk Analysis and Work Permit procedures ran on paper — filled by hand on site, slow to approve, hard to review, easy to lose.",
        },
        {
          title: "Role",
          body: "Sole designer — field research, usability testing, stakeholder alignment, flows, UI and the Design System.",
        },
        {
          title: "What changed",
          bullets: [
            "Paper forms became conditional flows for 8 high-risk scenarios",
            "Completion works offline, with evidence captured on site",
            "Supervisor approval moved into the workflow",
          ],
        },
        {
          title: "Outcome",
          body: "Developed and delivered: mobile + web workflows replacing the paper procedures.",
        },
      ],
    },

    {
      id: "field-research",
      eyebrow: "Field research",
      title: "Starting from the paper forms technicians actually use",
      layout: "text-first",
      imageColumns: 2,
      content: [
        "Before any screen, I went to a Comgás field site to see the process end to end — the printed \"PT\" (permissão de trabalho) form technicians fill by hand, and the risk-analysis checklist used before starting a task. Digitizing without seeing this would have missed how much of it happens standing up, outdoors, under time pressure.",
      ],
      images: [
        {
          src: "/images/comgas/paper-pt-form.png",
          alt: "Printed work permit form for work at height, filled by hand in the field",
          caption: "Paper PT form — handwritten in the field",
        },
        {
          src: "/images/comgas/risk-checklist-opt.jpg",
          alt: "Printed Comgás risk-analysis checklist",
          caption: "Printed risk-analysis checklist",
        },
      ],
    },

    {
      id: "decisions",
      kind: "cards",
      eyebrow: "Key decisions",
      title: "What had to be true for this to work in the field",
      content: [
        "The paper process wasn't just a form to digitize. It carried safety rules, different risk scenarios and a sign-off step — and it happens outdoors, under time pressure. These were the decisions that shaped the product.",
      ],
      cards: [
        {
          title: "One flow, eight risk scenarios",
          body: "Instead of one long generic form, the flow branches by activity. Each of the eight high-risk scenarios — such as work at height — shows only the questions and checks that apply to it, so nothing relevant is skipped and nothing irrelevant slows the technician down.",
        },
        {
          title: "Offline completion",
          body: "The app couldn't assume a connection on site. Technicians can complete the analysis and capture evidence offline, so the safety process never depends on signal.",
        },
        {
          title: "Supervisor approval in the workflow",
          body: "Approval, which used to depend on paper and signatures, became a step in the flow — the supervisor reviews the same structured record the technician submitted.",
        },
        {
          title: "Evidence and context at the source",
          body: "Photos, videos and contextual data are attached where the risk is registered, linking what happened in the field to what the office reviews.",
        },
      ],
    },

    {
      id: "alignment",
      eyebrow: "Stakeholders and testing",
      title: "Two versions of the same process — and one product",
      imageAlign: "center",
      imageMaxWidth: "360px",
      content: [
        "The office and the field didn't share the same picture of the paperwork. Managers had one idea of how it was done — the order of the steps and what came first — while the technicians filling it in on site had another. The product couldn't simply digitize either version; it had to reconcile both.",
        "I ran several alignment sessions with the safety area and field supervisors to bring those two views together into one flow — an order and set of priorities that matched how the work really happens and still gave the office the control it needed.",
        "The second challenge was adoption. For technicians, a new app could easily read as just another way to be monitored. So the flow had to be useful to them first — showing only what applied to their task, working offline and capturing evidence in the moment, instead of adding paperwork. Usability tests in the field checked that it held up in real conditions.",
      ],
      image: {
        src: "/images/comgas/field-visit-opt.jpg",
        alt: "Comgás field crew on site with a pipe-testing warning sign",
        caption: "On-site field visit",
      },
    },

    {
      id: "prototype",
      kind: "embed",
      eyebrow: "Evidence — the real file",
      title: "The real Figma file — flows, components, delivery",
      content: [
        "Embedding the actual \"Análise de Risco App\" file instead of another static summary — the full risk-analysis flow, the work-at-height flow, and the refined delivery components for EPIs.",
      ],
      embedUrl: `https://www.figma.com/embed?embed_host=share&url=${encodeURIComponent(figmaFileUrl)}`,
      embedTitle: "Análise de Risco App — Figma file",
      ctaHref: figmaFileUrl,
      ctaLabel: "Open the Figma file ↗",
    },

    {
      id: "risk-registration",
      eyebrow: "Risk registration",
      title: "Turning the paper checklist into a guided flow",
      layout: "text-first",
      content: [
        "The interface walks technicians through the same questions as the paper form — type of activity, depth, location, risk level — one at a time, so nothing gets skipped under time pressure, and the office team gets a structured submission instead of a scanned sheet.",
      ],
      image: {
        src: "/images/comgas/risk-registration-opt.jpg",
        alt: "Risk registration flow in the Comgás mobile app",
      },
    },

    {
      id: "evidence",
      eyebrow: "Evidence capture",
      title: "Connecting photos and field information",
      layout: "text-first",
      content: [
        "Technicians attach photo or video evidence directly to the risk submission — creating a reliable link between what happened in the field and what the office team reviews, instead of a paper form with no visual record.",
      ],
      image: {
        src: "/images/comgas/evidence-opt.jpg",
        alt: "Evidence capture screens with photos and videos",
      },
      imageMaxWidth: "900px",
    },

    {
      id: "dashboard",
      eyebrow: "Web",
      title: "Supporting review from the office",
      layout: "text-first",
      content: [
        "On the web side, submitted reports, evidence, status, location and operational data come together for managers — replacing scattered paper and spreadsheets with one review environment.",
      ],
      image: {
        src: "/images/comgas/dashboard-opt.jpg",
        alt: "Comgás web dashboard for reviewing field safety reports",
      },
    },

    {
      id: "mobile",
      eyebrow: "Mobile-first design",
      title: "A compact interface for fast field use",
      layout: "text-first",
      content: [
        "Screens stay focused — short forms, clear labels, visible progress — so a technician can complete the process outdoors without unnecessary friction.",
      ],
      image: {
        src: "/images/comgas/mobile-opt.jpg",
        alt: "Mobile screens of the Comgás field safety app",
      },
    },

    // TODO(Bruno): if possible, add an image of the Design System
    // (components/states) — e.g. /images/comgas/design-system-opt.jpg.
    {
      id: "delivery",
      eyebrow: "Design System and delivery",
      title: "Built to be implemented, not just presented",
      layout: "text-first",
      content: [
        "Eight scenarios sharing the same building blocks needed consistency more than new screens. I created the project's Design System — components for form steps, evidence capture and EPI checks — so each scenario could be assembled from the same parts.",
        "I handed the flows, components and documentation to development and supported homologation in the team's agile routine, until the app was delivered.",
      ],
    },

    {
      id: "outcome",
      kind: "checklist",
      eyebrow: "Outcome",
      title: "From handwritten forms to a working safety workflow",
      content: [
        "The product was developed and delivered, replacing paper-based Risk Analysis and Work Permit procedures with a connected mobile and web workflow — from the technician on site to the supervisor's approval and the office review.",
      ],
      checklistItems: [
        {
          title: "Developed and delivered",
          body: "Mobile app + web workflows replacing the paper procedures.",
        },
        {
          title: "Eight high-risk scenarios covered",
          body: "Conditional flows with offline completion, evidence capture and supervisor approval.",
        },
        {
          title: "Validated with stakeholders and users",
          body: "Rules aligned with the safety area and supervisors; flows tested with users.",
        },
        {
          title: "Design System",
          body: "Created for the project to keep the scenarios consistent and speed up delivery.",
        },
      ],
    },
  ],

  nextProject: {
    title: "Doutor Já",
    href: "/work/doutor-ja",
  },
};
---

<ProjectCase project={project} />
